## Why This Is Asked
This tests the ability to design robust per-topic schema validation with proper error handling and reliable offset management in Kafka consumer applications.

## Key Concepts
- JSON schema validation with type checking for required fields
- Per-topic dead-letter queue (DLQ) implementation
- Offset commit semantics with at-least-once delivery guarantees
- Idempotent message publishing and canary testing strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateUserEvent(rawMessage) {
  try {
    const payload = JSON.parse(rawMessage);
    if (typeof payload.userId === 'string' && 
        typeof payload.signupDate === 'string' && 
        typeof payload.metadata === 'object') {
      return { valid: true, payload };
    }
  } catch (error) {
    // Parsing error
  }
  return { valid: false, error: 'Invalid user event schema' };
}
```

## Testing Approach
Deploy canary with mixed valid/invalid messages to validate schema enforcement, DLQ routing, and offset commit behavior before full rollout.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-region privacy-preserving data bridges, balancing tenant isolation, latency, and regulatory needs.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation across regions
- Envelope encryption and KMS key management
- Cross-region data transfer with privacy-preserving aggregation
- Provenance and auditability
- Canary rollout and rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: envelope encrypt per-tenant data in-region
function encryptForTenant(data, tenantId, region) {
  const dataKey = kms.regional(region).generateDataKey(tenantId)
  const ciphertext = crypto.aes256GcmEncrypt(data, dataKey)
  const wrappedKey = kms(region).wrapKey(dataKey)
  return { ct: ciphertext, wk: wrappedKey, tenant: tenantId }
}
```

